   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.4. Increased capacity of Member States to provide adequate labour protection to workers in diverse forms of work arrangements, including on digital labour platforms, and in informal employment</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The amendments to the Labour Code adopted by the Parliament  (Law No. 13/2023 of 3 April 2023), established a set of measures to strengthen labour protections for workers in diverse forms of work.
The amendments were based on the &#x201C;Agenda for Decent Work and Valorisation of Young People in the Labour Market&#x201D;, with the following objectives: combat precarious work and consequently increase the value of wages; encourage social dialogue and collective bargaining; promote equality in the labour market between women and men; create conditions for a better balance between professional, family and personal life; strengthening monitoring mechanisms, particularly by cross-checking data to detect irregular situations more effectively. 
The Agenda, launched in 2022, also builds on the work of a set of publications - the Green Paper on Labour Relations and the Green Paper on the Future of Work -, and tripartite negotiations and agreements &#x2013; specifically the Agreement on Combating Precariousness, Reducing Labour Segmentation and Promoting Greater Dynamism in Collective Bargaining (2018)  and the Medium-Term Agreement to Improve Income, Wages and Competitiveness (2022) .
Some of the key highlights of the amended Law are:
- the provision of labour inspectors with the capacity to directly notify an employer when observing the existence of characteristic of an (non-celebrated) employment contract.
-the duration of temporary contracts &#x2013; through temporary work agencies - now has a maximum limit of 4 years when the same function is being performed (even if the employer is different). Temporary contracts can be renewed up to 4 times maximum. 
- The value of compensation for the termination of fixed-term contracts is increased to discourage the celebration of unjustified fixed-term contracts.
- the presumption of labour relationship regarding platform workers;
- the duty for the platform to inform the Labour Authority on the mechanisms of AI to monitor and supervise work; The Green Paper on the Future of Work was the kick-off to the public debate and the basis for social partners negotiations of the government&#x2019;s proposed Agenda. The Paper was greatly influenced by the ILO labour standards as well as by many ILO papers, worth of note the ones directly targeted at the analysis of Portuguese labour market which include policy recommendations, as well as those ILO publications focused on the future of work and digital labour platforms. 
The Green Book on the Future of Work quotes the economic and labour market assessments of the country - &#x201C;Portugal: A quick analysis of the impact of COVID-19 on the economy and labour market&#x201D; (ILO, 2020), coordinated by EMPLAB, as well as the more broader ILO diagnostic elaborated as technical assistance by the request of the Portuguese Ministry of Labour, Solidarity and Social Security  , which materialised in the report &#x201C;Decent Work in Portugal 2008-18 &#x2013; From crisis to recovery&#x201D; (ILO, 2018). 
The chapter dedicated to the work on digital platforms embedded not only context provided by the ILO report &#x201C;Digital labour platforms and the future of work&#x201D; (ILO, 2018), translated into Portuguese in 2020, but also guidelines for the extension of social protection to all kind of workers, independently of their contractual status. 
Beyond the publications, ILO experts contribute directly into several tripartite forums contributing to the evidence based national debate, including:
- participation on a panel with OECD and European Commission followed by a round table with the social partners, during the launch of the Project on a Green Paper on the Future of Work &#x2013;July 2020.
- the High-Level Conference on the Future of Work organized in March 2021 by the Portuguese Presidency of the Council of the European Union (1st Semester of 2021).</narrative>
   </description>
